<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Ocean Life and Conservation – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Ocean Life and Conservation</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Plants That Live in the Ocean</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Discuss plans for the future on a variety of topics. Interactive: Engage in debates on familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can discuss plans related to ocean conservation, educational visits, or recreational activities in Panama's coastal areas (e.g., "Next weekend, I am going to visit Punta Culebra to learn about sea turtle conservation." "My family is planning a trip to El Escudo de Veraguas to enjoy the beach and learn about its marine biodiversity." "In the future, I want to participate in a beach cleanup at Playa Venao to help reduce plastic waste.")
                Can engage in a debate about protecting marine life by presenting arguments and supporting evidence related to local conservation efforts (e.g., "I believe fishing limits near Coiba National Park are necessary to protect endangered fish species like the grouper and ensure sustainable fishing for future generations." "I think stricter regulations around Isla Iguanas are important because its coral reefs provide shelter for many species and attract eco-tourism during whale season, which benefits the community.")
                Can share future goals related to ocean conservation (e.g., "I am planning to join a group that cleans beaches to protect wildlife.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures of ocean plants found in Panama (e.g., different types of seaweed, mangrove forests, specific coral reefs like those near Isla Iguana). Students work in pairs to name the plants they recognize and share one fact or observation about them. The teacher asks open-ended questions like, "What do you see in the pictures?" "Where do these plants live?" "Why are they important?" This activates prior knowledge from previous lessons and encourages initial speaking using core vocabulary (e.g., <i>ocean, plants, coral reefs, mangroves, shoreline</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using target vocabulary and grammar to describe ocean plants and their importance. For example, "These are <i>mangroves</i>. They <i>grow</i> near the <i>shoreline</i>. <i>Mangroves protect</i> the land from <i>waves</i> and are <i>habitat</i> for young <i>fish</i>." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of multisyllabic words like "<i>ecosystem</i>" and "<i>biodiversity</i>" and compound words like "<i>shoreline</i>," focusing on correct stress. The teacher also demonstrates how to form simple questions and answers about conservation plans, linking to the specific objective.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms and concepts related to the theme.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: "Are coral reefs just pretty rocks, or are they homes for many <i>species</i>?" (Targeting <i>habitat</i>, <i>species</i>, <i>ecological importance</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: "If we say a species is '<i>endangered</i>,' does that mean there are many of them, or only a few left?" (Targeting <i>endangered</i>, <i>conservation</i> need).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Ocean Life and Conservation"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio recording (1-2 minutes) of a "Public Service Announcement (PSA)" or a brief interview snippet from a local Panamanian environmentalist discussing the importance of marine plants and proposing conservation actions. The material focuses on the ecological importance of <i>coral reefs</i> and <i>mangroves</i>, highlighting how they <i>protect</i> the <i>coastline</i> and foster <i>biodiversity</i>. The register is informative and persuasive.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student comprehends main ideas and specific details in short spoken messages and presentations related to familiar topics, such as environmental conservation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio twice.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> After the first listening, "What is the main idea of this announcement/interview? Is it about tourism, pollution, or protecting ocean plants?" (Students raise hands or give a short answer).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Specific details (True/False):</b> After the second listening, students answer True/False questions in pairs based on the audio. For example: "True or False: The speaker says mangroves help create <i>habitat</i> for young <i>fish</i>." "True or False: The speaker suggests that <i>pollution</i> is not a problem for <i>coral reefs</i>." Students discuss their answers with their partner, practicing speaking.</p>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using Present Simple for describing facts about ocean life, Present Continuous for ongoing actions/threats, and Modals (<i>should, must, need to</i>) for suggestions and obligations regarding conservation. Vocabulary focus on <i>ocean, plants, species, coral reefs, marine life, ecosystem, pollution, habitat, biodiversity, seaweed, plankton, conservation, coastal areas, preservation, mangroves, protected zones</i>, and verbs like <i>protect, conserve, preserve, sustain</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides students with incomplete sentences on a worksheet. Students orally complete the sentences using target vocabulary and grammar, then read their completed sentences aloud to a partner. E.g., "<i>Coral reefs are</i> home to many ______ (species/pollution)." "We ______ (should/are) <i>protecting</i> our <i>mangroves</i> from <i>coastal pollution</i>." "The sea grass ________ (is growing/grow) near the <i>shoreline</i>, providing shelter."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled Practice - Guided Discussion):</b> In pairs, students receive a set of sentence starters related to ocean plants and conservation (e.g., "I believe that <i>mangroves are</i> important because...", "To <i>protect coral reefs</i>, we <i>should</i>...", "<i>Plastic pollution is threatening</i>...", "My family <i>is planning</i> to..."). They take turns completing the sentences orally, using the target language. The teacher circulates, providing feedback on grammar and pronunciation, especially stress on "<i>environmental</i>" and "<i>endangered</i>," and minimal pairs like /iː/ in "<i>reef</i>" and /ɪ/ in "<i>riff</i>."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Opinion Sharing):</b> Students work in new pairs. They receive cards with statements like "<i>Marine protected areas are necessary for all ocean species.</i>" or "<i>Cleaning beaches is the best way to fight ocean pollution.</i>" Students must agree or disagree with the statement and briefly explain why, using at least one modal verb and target vocabulary. E.g., "I agree. We <i>should establish</i> more <i>protected zones</i> to help <i>preserve biodiversity</i>."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Ocean Life and Conservation"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Debate / Group Discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Task:</b> Students work in small groups of 4-5. Each group is given a specific "Panamanian coastal area" (e.g., Coiba National Park, Isla Iguana Wildlife Refuge, Bay of Panama mangroves) and a question to debate: "What are the most effective ways to protect ocean plants and the <i>marine life</i> they support in [specific area]?" Groups are divided into two teams: one arguing for "Strict Regulations and <i>Protected Zones</i>" and the other for "Community Involvement and Sustainable Practices." Each team must prepare 2-3 arguments, using the target vocabulary, Present Simple, Present Continuous, and Modals (<i>should, must, need to</i>) to propose conservation actions and describe ecological importance. They will engage in a mini-debate within their group, presenting their arguments and responding to counter-arguments. The focus is on fluent interaction and effective communication of their viewpoints. (e.g., "I believe fishing limits near Coiba National Park <i>are necessary</i> to <i>protect endangered fish species</i> like the grouper and <i>ensure sustainable fishing</i> for future generations.")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the group debates (Performance stage), the teacher uses an observation checklist to note student participation, use of target language, and fluency. Additionally, students provide peer feedback using a simple checklist, evaluating their group members on their ability to present arguments and engage in discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (e.g., stress in <i>ecosystem, biodiversity</i>, minimal pairs reef/riff).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least 5 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>mangroves, coral reefs, species, habitat, conservation, pollution, protected zones</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of modals for suggestions/obligations (e.g., "<i>We should protect</i>...", "<i>We need to clean</i>...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of arguments related to ocean plant protection.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active and collaborative interaction within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short self-reflection before leaving.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or phrase you used effectively in today's debate?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you learned about protecting ocean plants today?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now discussing plans for ocean conservation with your classmates?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific part of speaking or debating in English do you want to improve next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What speaking strategies or activities worked best to encourage student participation and use of target language? Did students effectively engage in debates on familiar topics and discuss future plans? What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., more sentence frames, specific prompt cards, additional pronunciation practice) are needed for students to further develop their productive and interactive speaking skills, especially concerning vocabulary integration and coherent argumentation?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson's debate on ocean plant conservation will serve as a strong foundation for Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), where students will write a short persuasive paragraph or a "Public Service Announcement (PSA)" script, consolidating the arguments and vocabulary practiced today into a written product.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>